when we think of religion in the Middle  East we tend to view it in a rather  simplified and generalizing way in the  Middle East people are Muslim basically  and while yes there are of course a lot  of Muslims in the Middle East there is a  whole host of other religious minorities  that have lived there historically and  today and not just jews and christians  and one of those less known religious  minority groups are known as the  mandaeans man bei ism can be described  as a monotheistic religion with strong  dualistic tendencies similar to other  movements like Gnosticism it shares a  lot of elements with other religions  like Judaism Christianity and so Astron  ISM but of course constitutes its own  unique tradition and religion the word  monday ism or Monday ins comes from the  word manda or Monday which means  knowledge in the old language called  Monday in a Semitic language that only  survives today through the Liturgy of  the Monday and priests in Arabic and  Muslim context they have also often been  known as Suba which basically means  those who baptized this also connects it  to the term sabaeans which is a  mysterious religious group mentioned in  the Quran alongside Christianity and  Judaism as the people of the book some  historically and today have therefore  concluded that the sabaeans in the Quran  are in fact the mandaeans  the mundanes have for most of history  been confined or located in the southern  regions of Iraq along the Euphrates and  Tigris Rivers but their actual  geographic origins are rather uncertain  and unknown many believe that they  originates in in Palestine in the first  three centuries ad and maybe in  connection to certain Jewish groups  along the Jordan River that put a heavy  emphasis on the practice of baptism as a  ritual practice now this of course sort  of very much connects them to movements  like the Essenes and with John the  Baptist in fact John the Baptist who  very famously baptized Jesus in the New  Testament plays a very central role to  the Mende and religion he isn't  considered a founder by any means but he  has every central role as a kind of  prophet and one of the  main sort of propagators interestingly  while they share many aspects and  mythological backgrounds of the  Abrahamic faiths they attended to have a  different perspective on it a lot of the  time Adam the first man that God created  is venerated and Monday ISM is  considered to have originated with him  so similar to for example dis LOM they  thus view themselves as the true  original religion of mankind and all  other religions as sort of deviations  from this original religion they also  venerate Adams son Seth and one of  Noah's sons Shem as transmitters of the  Mende and faith but this also means that  all other biblical prophets mentioned in  the Old Testaments aside from these few  are considered false similarly while  they may affirm that John the Baptist  did baptize Jesus they consider the  Christian faith to be false and Jesus as  a kind of heretic and misleader  who deviated from John's true religion  we should always avoid thinking about  religions as or through a kind of  Christian framework as having a founder  and a sacred scripture for example but  there are a few texts that sort of  contain a lot of these basic monday and  teachings the most prominent of these  can be said to be a book called guinness  Arriba or a simply ginza which is a  collection of theological historical and  ritual teachings there is also the  mandiant book of john which is thought  to have been authored by John the  Baptist himself and contains stories  about his life and teachings and many  other scriptures of varying sorts as  well scholars disagree on when these  texts were written or compiled some  placing them or at least a few of them  after the coming of Islam in these 7th  century and others placed them a couple  of centuries before that now if we were  to talk about the beliefs or a theology  or cosmology of this religion we need to  make some things clear first like with  many other religions there isn't one  clear story or agreed-upon version of  the cosmological beliefs often with  religions that don't share the sort of  institutionalized structure of a  Catholic Church for example there isn't  the same emphasis put on orthodoxy or a  certain orthodoxy that has been sort of  preserved as historian of religions  Jordan  Jacobson Buckley points out quote the  sheer mass of variations in the creation  mythology makes it impossible to appoint  one specific version as the original and  quote in any case the basic common  elements of the religious beliefs are as  I said earlier strongly dualistic with a  huge emphasis on light versus darkness  purity verses on cleanliness and so on  they are a monotheistic faith as they  believe in one Almighty transcendent God  which they call higher MB and the  highest sort of part of the cosmos a  pure world of lights in which this God  resides is also home to other sort of  spiritual luminous beings and the most  important of these is a spirit that they  refer to as manda and hai which means  knowledge of life and is it is  particularly through with this spiritual  being or similar to an angel almost it  is through this being that the Mende in  teachings and religions the religion has  been conveyed throughout history to  different prophets like Seth or to John  the Baptist there is then a kind of  hierarchy of existence from this highest  pure world of lights right down to the  lowest sort of dark bowels of creation  were evil and sort of darkness rule and  in between these two extremes the  material world of human existence exists  or is situated and contains elements of  both of these extremes according to some  scholars Monday ISM is here rather  similar to Gnosticism as it views the  human material body as created by a kind  of Demiurge through the powers of evil  the material body is therefore seen as a  kind of prison of the soul and the souls  goal in life is to escape this body  after death and make its way to the  higher world of pure and light but how  is this achieved well this brings us of  course to the practices of this religion  as I mentioned there is no orthodoxy in  Monday ISM this is actually a good  example to show that for the most part  religions are usually something that we  do rather than something we believe the  Chiefs of the Mandaeans are strongly  varied but the ritual practices are  pretty consistent throughout we could  therefore classify Monday ISM primarily  as a religion of orthopraxy those rights  practice rather than orthodoxy right  belief in this cosmological scheme that  I just described water is the only thing  in the material world that is entirely  pure through water one can remove sin  and uncleanliness and give the soul like  kind of rebirth similar to Christian and  early Jewish ideas this is why the  ritual of baptism called mascoutah is  perhaps the most central practice in  Monday ISM contrary to Christianity  where one is baptized only once the  Monday ins traditionally are baptized  once every week on Sundays one is to be  baptized in running water  traditionally usually one of the two  rivers in Iraq and this has created some  problems for a few diaspora communities  today through baptism the soul is  purified which will help it in its  journey to the world of lights after  death the baptism is performed by a  class of priests and usually in a place  of worship called a Mandi I'm sort of  usually placed beside the river a second  important ritual is called meseta a kind  of ascension of the soul ceremony this  can take many different forms but is  primarily performed to help departed  souls on the journey to the world of  lights which is plagued by demons and  other nasty things mandaeans also pray  daily but the number of prayers can vary  everything from three to five or seven  times a day as you might expect also the  idea of purity and cleanliness is very  important to the religion and mandaeans  have been in are still rather strict  about not allowing anyone to marry  outside of the religion they have also  traditionally not allowed people to  convert to mundane ism which has partly  led to its sort of decline in numbers  other ethical ideas and practices  include a kind of pacifism mandaeans  aren't allowed to carry any weapons for  example and giving to charity and also a  ritual fast at last for 36 days the  Mandaeans have always basically been a  minority in this region which hasn't  been ruled by many empires and  kingdoms throughout history and as usual  with minority groups this means they  haven't always been treated all that  well they were persecuted and oppressed  under the sustain ian's Persian Empire  before the arrival of the Arabs and  Islamic sort of conquerors as I  mentioned earlier because of their  identification with the sabaeans in the  Quran they have usually been identified  with the people of the book and thus  have retained certain rights and freedom  of religion and you could say sort of a  certain level of autonomy throughout the  Muslim period but of course this doesn't  mean that they weren't persecuted from  time to time by the Muslim rulers as  well in no period has this been more  prominent perhaps than in the last  century or so with the increasing  instability in the Middle East both  politically and in terms of rising  religious fundamentalism during the rule  of the Baath Party of Saddam Hussein and  Iraq and especially after his execution  and the war in 2003 the Mende ins were  given such a hard time that the majority  have by now emigrated to regions outside  of the Middle East it's estimated that  around 80 percents of mandarins live in  diaspora communities around the world  which is a rather recent development  this is that the certain conflicts  within the community regarding things  like integration and how much the  religious practices and beliefs can be  modified according to new contexts in a  place in like Sweden for example which  is rather cold for maybe one half of the  year and in which even the waters  outside can freeze this of course  creates problems regarding how to gain  access to running water for the the  baptism rituals and this has led to some  internal conflicts were some allow  compromises like indoor pools while  others don't want to make this  compromise similarly intermarriage with  other religious groups have been heated  discussions as has been the question of  people are allowed to convert the  mundane religion is also strongly  connected to ethnicity much like  religions like the Yazidi store to some  degree Judaism as well and your identity  as a mundane is primarily determined by  your parents being mandiant and so this  is the reason why conversion has usually  been neglected it's hard to say how many  Monday ins exist in the world today but  estimates place them everywhere between  forty thousand to a hundred thousand  people with as I said eighty percent of  them living outside of the Middle East  today so what do you think have you ever  heard of Monday ISM and what are your  thoughts on this fascinating religion  let's discuss in the comments and if you  want to support the channel you can like  the video and subscribe I'll see you  next time  [Music]  you
 

